WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:01.770
- Let me jog, I've been very lucky.

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:03.110
Something's got to be done.

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:03.943
- [Narrator] From the beginning

00:00:03.943 --> 00:00:05.170
of former New York City Mayor

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:07.350
Michael Bloomberg's presidential campaign,

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.450
he bet on Super Tuesday,

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.980
seeing it as an opportunity
to enter the race

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:14.957
and scoop up a substantial
number of delegates.

00:00:14.957 --> 00:00:17.107
- I'm in it to win it, and.
(crowd cheering)

00:00:17.107 --> 00:00:19.130
- [Narrator] That didn't pay off.

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:21.000
Bloomberg ended his big for president

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.710
after winning only one nominating
contest in America Samoa.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:29.710
- I entered the race for
president to defeat Donald Trump,

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:33.990
and today, I am leaving the
race for the same reason,

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:36.600
to defeat Donald Trump, because staying in

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.480
would make it more difficult
to achieve that goal.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.490
- [Narrator] Tuesday's results
set up what looks to be more

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:42.770
like a two-person race

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:45.040
between former Vice President Joe Biden

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.350
and Senator Bernie Sanders,

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:48.980
but if you were looking
at your Facebook feed

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:52.240
ahead of Super Tuesday, it
probably didn't feel that way.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:53.640
- [Woman] We have a choice to make.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.730
- [Narrator] Bloomberg
spent at least $620 million

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:59.350
on building his campaign
and spreading his message

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:00.450
before dropping out.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:02.870
About 75 million of that went to digital

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:04.720
where his social media strategy

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.400
was both unique and controversial.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.570
At the center of those
efforts was Hawkfish,

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:11.930
a digital tech firm Bloomberg owns.

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:13.690
It was set up not just for him,

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:15.590
but to help Democrats generally.

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:19.720
- So Mike Bloomberg created
Hawkfish in spring of 2019,

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:22.470
and a lot of Bloomberg's
advisors really believed

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:24.450
that Trump won the 2016 election

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:27.160
because of their very
strong digital effort.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:28.580
- [Narrator] So the firm's primary focus

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:31.600
was on digital advertising
on Facebook and Google.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:34.250
Bloomberg ran ads on issues
such as climate change

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:35.830
and gun safety reform,

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:38.750
though most were focused on
attacking President Trump,

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:40.330
and that's something that could outlast

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:41.850
Bloomberg's bid for president.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:43.240
Hawkfish also experimented

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:45.840
with new ways of reaching younger voters.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.850
There was this meme or
Bloomberg's face on a meatball

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:50.790
and this one that the Bloomberg campaign

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:53.250
paid a popular Instagram account to post.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:55.640
Separately, there was this doctored video

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:57.310
where Bloomberg asks--

00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:58.300
- I'm the only one here I think

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:00.623
that's ever started a
business, is that fair?

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:04.850
- [Narrator] And the other
candidates appear to fall silent.

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:07.080
- So a bunch of Democratic
strategists and others

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:09.460
said that that video was misinformation.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:12.300
The Bloomberg campaign said
it was tongue-in-cheek.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:13.780
- [Narrator] More controversial, however,

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:15.360
was the Bloomberg campaign's use

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.700
of what it calls deputy digital
organizers in California.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.030
The Wall Street Journal reported

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:22.180
that the campaign hired
hundreds of workers

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:25.610
to post regularly on their
personal social media accounts

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:27.150
in support of Bloomberg

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:30.440
and then send text messages
to their friends about him.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:31.830
- While it's totally common

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:34.170
for different political
campaigns to ask their supporters

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:36.140
to spread the message by word of mouth,

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.750
it's quite unusual to
pay thousands of dollars

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:40.200
to get that support.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.180
Then it gets into this murky
area of what the rules are,

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:45.230
so do you have to disclose if you're paid

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:46.590
for writing a post?

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:48.290
Yes, generally, you have to,

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:49.770
and the Federal Trade Commission

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.590
says you have to for businesses,

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:53.820
but the Federal Election Commission

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:55.970
which oversees campaign finance law

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:58.970
does not yet have a
specific rule on that front.

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:01.210
- [Narrator] A lot of tech
platforms like Facebook,

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:03.840
which owns Instagram, Google, and Twitter

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.950
have tried to set their own
rules for political content,

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:08.250
but their policies differ

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:12.120
on when and exactly what
a user has to disclose.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.810
- We are in this ultra-digital
social media-infused climate,

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:19.180
and a lot of the rules have
not caught up to the content.

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:20.540
- [Narrator] Now the question is,

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:23.050
will Bloomberg keep
spending against Trump?

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:25.860
When ending his bid, he endorsed Biden.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:29.220
Hawkfish is expected to keep
working on behalf of Democrats.

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:31.050
- Hawkfish has done work before

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:32.850
that doesn't involve Mike Bloomberg.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:36.150
In the 2018 midterms, it worked
on state legislative races

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:38.800
in states like Kentucky and Virginia,

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:40.590
so we think it's possible
that they could do

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:42.170
that type of work again,

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:44.440
and sources have said
that they will stay open

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:46.040
throughout the general election.

